window.SPL_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Build AI workflows in minutes, not hours","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html","text":"Turn Markdown into runnable AI workflows — natural-language steps alongside deterministic code, validation, and human approval.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Opt-in Types","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#opt-in-types","text":"Language is dynamic but has full support for opt-in static type checker. There are supported primitive types, structs, classes and dynamic catch-all objects. Functions are first class citizens.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Why SPL?","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#why-spl","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"How a run works","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#how-a-run-works","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Non-goals","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#non-goals","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Where next","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#where-next","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Getting started","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html","text":"Install the CLI, configure OpenAI, Anthropic or Google credentials, then write and refine a workflow in a short edit-run loop.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Install","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#install","text":"Use Node.js >=24.15.0. Native BetterTUI packages support Windows, macOS and Linux on x64/arm64. If native rendering cannot start, the CLI diagnoses the problem and keeps complete plain logs. spl login \u003cprovider> prompts for an API key without echoing it and saves a separate key for each provider in ~/.spl/credentials.json (override the folder with SPL_HOME). It also selects that provider as the default. If the provider's environment key is already set, login selects the provider without prompting or copying the environment secret into the file. Keys are configured locally; login does not make a billable request or verify account access. Alternatively, set OPENAI_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY. Google also accepts GOOGLE_API_KEY and GEMINI_API_KEY, in that order after the canonical name. Environment keys take precedence over saved keys. A single configured provider is detected automatically. With multiple providers, select one with --provider, SPL_PROVIDER, a model such as --model google/gemini-2.5-flash, or a saved login default. See Models and authentication for precedence and defaults.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Write a workflow","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#write-a-workflow","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Document structure","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#document-structure","text":"Fences and blockquotes run in document order in one shared scope. Indented SPL blocks create lexical child scopes. Blocks use : plus a newline and exactly four spaces per level, with pass for an empty body. Use object: for dictionaries (object() when empty); old braces and inline suites are no longer accepted. See the typing migration guide and block syntax and migration. success, fail and a top-level return end the whole document.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"The iteration loop","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#the-iteration-loop","text":"Replay needs the same model requests in the same order. After changing a prompt, record a new trace. Explicit tool calls still run during replay; see traces and replay.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"VS Code extension","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#vs-code-extension","text":"Install from Marketplace: SPL The extension in vscode/ adds parser/type-checker diagnostics, completions, hover, go to definition (F12), references (Shift+F12), safe rename (F2), signature help, document symbols, open-document workspace symbol search, folding and semantic highlighting. It supports workflow Markdown, standalone .spl editor files and SPL notebook cells; ordinary Markdown keeps its normal language behavior. Type splworkflow for a new workflow document. SPL fences and @name markers are highlighted in the editor, and SPL fences also receive Markdown preview highlighting. In the Markdown text editor, navigation and rename share top-level declarations across SPL fences and blockquote references, while respecting inner lexical scopes. Raw strings, escaped interpolations and commentary are not rewritten. Rename rejects identifier conflicts and reference capture. The language server only analyzes source and never calls models or tools. Static analysis does not infer names introduced by natural-language actions or dynamic member types. References and rename stay inside the current document; workspace symbols cover open documents only. Notebook analysis is per cell, independent of shared runtime execution state. Use the Markdown text editor for static navigation across fences. The CLI still runs Markdown workflows, not standalone .spl scratch files. See the extension guide for features, shortcuts, analysis boundaries and development instructions. Or install from repository:","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Run the tests","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#run-the-tests","text":"Tests are offline. To include authenticated live tests in PowerShell, run $env:SPL_LIVE_TESTS='1'; npm test, then remove the variable.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Language","page":"Language","href":"language.html","text":"SPL is a small Python-like language with colon-and-indentation blocks. This page covers the code inside spl fences: values, bindings, control flow, functions, built-ins and exact tools.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Block syntax and migration","page":"Language","href":"language.html#block-syntax-and-migration","text":"Breaking change: blocks use : followed by a newline and an indented, nonempty body, not { ... }. To migrate, replace each opening block brace with :, move any inline body onto its own indented lines, and remove the closing brace. Align elif, else and except with their opening statement. Replace empty bodies with pass; inline suites are not supported. Use exactly four spaces per indentation level. Tabs in indentation are prohibited. Unexpected indentation, a missing indented body, and a dedent that does not match a prior level are syntax errors. Blank and comment-only lines are ignored: they neither open nor close a block and do not count as a body. Semicolons can separate simple statements only, never block headers or compound statements. Dictionary literals {...} are removed: use object: with indented key: value entries, and object() for an empty dictionary. String interpolation {expr} and JSON wire syntax are unchanged. Newlines inside () and [] suspend ordinary indentation; explicit object suites still delimit their entries. Explicit backslash continuation remains supported. Statement blocks retain lexical child scopes, not Python assignment semantics; dictionary keys do not form a lexical scope. See the manual typing migration guide.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Values and strings","page":"Language","href":"language.html#values-and-strings","text":"Values include None, True/False, numbers, strings, lists, dictionaries with string keys, functions, immutable type descriptors and nominal class instances. Simple statements end at a newline or ;. # starts a comment, and a trailing \\ continues a line. Escapes: \\n \\t \\r \\0 \\\\ \\' \\\" \\{ \\}. Python-style operators work as expected: + - * / // % **, comparisons, in/not in, and/or/not, a if cond else b, indexing, slicing with negative indexes, and d.key as a shorthand for d['key'].","group":"The language"},{"title":"Bindings and scopes","page":"Language","href":"language.html#bindings-and-scopes","text":"let declares a name in the current block; plain assignment updates the nearest existing declaration. Every block is a child scope, so inner let shadows outer names and does not leak out. Each loop iteration gets fresh variables. Declare a result before a block when the block should set it: let answer = None and then answer = ... inside.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Control flow and expressions","page":"Language","href":"language.html#control-flow-and-expressions","text":"if/elif/else, for, while, break, continue and pass use Python-like layout, but keep SPL's lexical scopes. for k, v in items(d) unpacks pairs. Blocks and if are expressions that yield their last expression, or None. Anonymous value-producing blocks begin with a bare :, as in result above. object() is an empty dictionary; a block containing only pass yields None.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Language","href":"language.html#functions","text":"def defines a function with lexical closures and recursion. Functions return None unless they use an explicit return.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Gradual typing","page":"Language","href":"language.html#gradual-typing","text":"Annotations are optional enforced contracts, not advisory lint hints. Untyped bindings remain dynamic. Known typed violations block execution before model calls; unknown values are validated at typed boundaries without coercion, defaults or field removal. Built-in types and existing type declarations remain available after if, loops, and try/except blocks. An untyped alias used as an annotation must still identify the same type on every possible control-flow path.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Types at a glance","page":"Language","href":"language.html#types-at-a-glance","text":"Type Accepted values --- --- int Signed 64-bit integers; checked arithmetic, no overflow wrapping float Finite binary64 numbers, distinct from int str Strings char Strings containing exactly one Unicode scalar value bool True or False , not integers None The null value, not an absent field object Dynamic string-keyed dictionaries, not nominal class instances list[T] Lists whose elements satisfy T A \\ B Values satisfying either member; narrow before member-specific operations fn(T1, T2) -> R Callables with the declared parameter and return contracts Typed literals select the representation: let count: int = 2, but let ratio: float = 2.0. Neither let count: int = 2.0 nor let ratio: float = 2 is valid. Untyped numbers keep their existing representation; use int(value) or float(value) explicitly to enter typed numeric code. int(...) rejects fractions, booleans and out-of-range values; conversion to float can lose integer precision. Mixed int/float arithmetic requires explicit conversion. Integer / produces a float. Counts from len and range remain untyped numbers.","group":"The language","signatures":["int","float","str","char","bool","None","object","list[T]","A | B","fn(T1, T2) -> R"]},{"title":"Transparent type aliases","page":"Language","href":"language.html#transparent-type-aliases","text":"Declare read-only, transparent aliases with optional semantic advice: Aliases accept the same values and operations as their underlying types, without implicit conversion. Advice is included in generated schemas' descriptions, including when an alias is used as a field or list element; it does not enforce extra constraints. Aliasing an alias inherits its advice, with newly supplied keys overriding inherited keys. Field advice is appended separately. type(value) remains the type-name builtin.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Enums, structs and functions","page":"Language","href":"language.html#enums-structs-and-functions","text":"Enums accept matching strings directly and are subtypes of str. Structs are structural: equivalent field contracts are interchangeable. Fields are exact and required; nullable does not mean absent, so note above must be present even when it is None. Metadata such as str [min=2, desc='Name'] is model advice, not a validity constraint. Use assert for business rules, or explicit JSON Schema for strict data constraints. Function parameters, results and callable fields are checked at their typed boundaries. A union value can be assigned or passed to another union, including a list element contract, when every source alternative is accepted by the destination. Member order does not matter; an alternative missing from the destination must be narrowed first.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Validation, narrowing and mutation","page":"Language","href":"language.html#validation-narrowing-and-mutation","text":"validate(value, Type) checks values and returns a typed view, or fails. For a class target it explicitly converts structural field data into a nominal instance. isinstance(value, Type) tests without converting and narrows within a branch: Neither operation converts untyped numbers. isinstance never converts structural data into a class. validate(data, Class) checks all declared fields and creates a complete instance without running the constructor, including for ordinary classes. Missing or extra fields fail; no defaults or numeric conversions are inserted. Class conversion also applies to nested fields, lists and union members; distinct ambiguous conversions fail. Converted field dictionaries and containers are copied rather than rewriting the source. Successful tests and validation install persistent contracts on mutable values. These survive untyped aliases, index/member writes and bulk mutators. For example, an untyped alias cannot append an integer to a list[str]. Mutable contracts are invariant: list[Color] cannot be used as list[str]. Failed bulk mutations leave the whole value unchanged. Narrowing expires after relevant assignment or potentially mutating calls.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Classes and structural projections","page":"Language","href":"language.html#classes-and-structural-projections","text":"Classes have nominal identity, declared fields, and implicitly bound self in methods. Normal construction calls the constructor named after the class and must initialize every field. Use hydratable class only when constructor-free JSON hydration is safe: struct(Person) describes structural field data, not a Person instance. To explicitly turn that data into an instance, use validate(data, Person): This conversion bypasses constructor normalization and side effects. Direct schema=GeneratedPerson still requires a hydratable class declaration. object(person) makes a deep snapshot and drops named function properties. Validate that snapshot against struct(Person) when a checked field view is needed; validation rejects missing required fields, including callable fields dropped by projection. Use explicit projection before serializing class instances. See Schemas and repairs for typed model output, typed-data.md for an offline workflow, and Typing migration for the full migration rules.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Runtime inputs: $env and $args","page":"Language","href":"language.html#runtime-inputs-env-and-args","text":"$env.NAME or $env['NAME'] reads a process environment variable as a string. $args[0], $args[1], … read the CLI's repeated --arg values. Missing variables and out-of-range indexes return None. Both are read-only snapshots and are never persisted by stores.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Logging","page":"Language","href":"language.html#logging","text":"log(value, ...) writes to stderr at info level. log(level=debug)(value) selects error, info or debug. Debug output appears only with --log-level debug; --silent hides all logs. Objects and lists are logged as indented JSON, including when mixed with text arguments. Every continuation line carries the same log tag. Terminal output colors JSON tokens; redirected logs are plain text by default. See console formatting.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Built-in functions","page":"Language","href":"language.html#built-in-functions","text":"Function Description --- --- len(x) type(x) Length; type name str(x) int(x) float(x) bool(x) list(x) Conversions object() object(x) Empty dictionary; deep dynamic-data snapshot validate(x, T) isinstance(x, T) Validation with explicit struct-to-class conversion; non-converting type testing struct(Class) Structural descriptor for declared class fields, not nominal hydration range(stop) range(start, stop[, step]) List of integers abs(x) round(x[, digits]) min(...) max(...) sum(list) Numbers sorted(list[, reverse]) reversed(list) enumerate(list) zip(a, b, ...) Sequences any(list) all(list) Truthiness checks keys(d) values(d) items(d) Dict views as lists json_parse(text) json_stringify(value[, indent]) Strict JSON log(...) Logging, see above Methods: Type Methods --- --- String upper lower strip lstrip rstrip title capitalize isdigit split splitlines join replace startswith endswith find count List append extend insert pop remove index count reverse sort copy clear Dict keys values items get pop update copy clear","group":"The language","signatures":["len(x)","type(x)","str(x)","int(x)","float(x)","bool(x)","list(x)","object()","object(x)","validate(x, T)","isinstance(x, T)","struct(Class)","range(stop)","range(start, stop[, step])","abs(x)","round(x[, digits])","min(...)","max(...)","sum(list)","sorted(list[, reverse])","reversed(list)","enumerate(list)","zip(a, b, ...)","any(list)","all(list)","keys(d)","values(d)","items(d)","json_parse(text)","json_stringify(value[, indent])","log(...)"]},{"title":"Exact tools","page":"Language","href":"language.html#exact-tools","text":"Tools are ordinary functions that never call a model. Paths are relative to --cwd, and every call is checked against active restrictions. Function Result --- --- read_file(path) File contents as UTF-8 text write_file(path, content) Writes the file and returns a confirmation web_fetch(url) Response body as text (30 s timeout, up to 5 redirects) web_search(query) Search results as text shell(command) Combined stdout/stderr; a nonzero exit raises tool_error (120 s timeout) shell runs through the platform shell (cmd.exe on Windows, /bin/sh elsewhere). External state can still change between runs; \"exact\" means no model is involved.","group":"The language","signatures":["read_file(path)","write_file(path, content)","web_fetch(url)","web_search(query)","shell(command)"]},{"title":"Natural language & models","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html","text":"Blockquotes let you write a step in plain language; ask, think and agent call the model from code. This page covers both, and how to check what comes back.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Blockquotes","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#blockquotes","text":"A blockquote is an agent command, not code to compile into SPL. The model sees the instruction, visible bindings and function signatures, active guidance and restrictions, and the available tools. It acts directly on the shared document scope through tool calls. For a simple generation task, the comparable code is: Blockquotes have these tools in addition to configured MCP tools: Tool Purpose --- --- load_variable(name) Read a shared variable or read-only $args ; declared types return their complete JSON Schema save_variable(name, value, schema?) Create or update a shared value; optionally use a declared type/schema name or inline JSON Schema call_function(name, args) Invoke an existing SPL function or builtin with positional JSON arguments get_env(name) Read one read-only environment value; missing values return null ask_human(question) Ask for human input and return the answer directly read_file , write_file , web_fetch , web_search , shell Use the existing exact runtime tools restrict(kind, value) Narrow restrictions for the rest of the command, including function and MCP calls set_guidance(kind, text) Add enforce / forbid guidance for this command and its nested model calls success(value) , fail(reason) End the whole document These shared-scope tools are specific to blockquotes. Explicit agent expressions retain their existing runtime/MCP tool set; ask and think still have no tools. call_function decodes JSON arguments against parameter annotations, including nested lists and records. For example, JSON 15 becomes an SPL int for a parameter declared n: int; unannotated parameters keep ordinary dynamic values. save_variable also decodes against an existing binding's type annotation. This does not relax validation of ordinary SPL values: numeric strings, fractional integers, invalid shapes, and ambiguous decoding targets still fail. Tool arguments are already parsed JSON, so integer values outside JavaScript's safe integer range are rejected when decoding to int, rather than risking a rounded value. Numeric unions interpret integral parsed numbers as int and fractional numbers or negative zero as float; the original decimal/exponent spelling is no longer available at this boundary. For @validate[Review], the agent can inspect Review with load_variable(\"Review\"). The returned schema includes nested fields, lists, and enums; \u003ctype Review> in the shared bindings overview is only a display label. The agent then saves with save_variable(name=\"review\", value=..., schema=\"Review\"). The runtime resolves the declared type, validates and decodes the JSON, and hydrates any opted-in classes, including nested ones. Structs and typed containers retain their contracts; constructor-only classes are rejected. An inline JSON Schema object or boolean, or the name of a binding holding one, keeps the existing validation-only behavior without class hydration. Invalid values fail before saving. @name marks a binding or operation (@notes, @success, @read_file), not text substitution. Plain language works too. Common conventions guide the agent's tool choices: Marker Agent behavior --- --- @ENFORCE text / @FORBID text Follow prompt guidance; use set_guidance for nested model calls @RESTRICT fs['./src'] exec['git'] Narrow the command's restrictions with restrict @validate[Schema] Inspect Schema , then pass its name to save_variable for validation and typed decoding; invalid values stop the command @HUMAN question Call ask_human ; the shell prompts or consumes the next --human answer @SUCCESS , @fail End the document For a list or union schema, declare a named alias first, such as type Reviews = list[Review], and use @validate[Reviews]. The tool resolves binding names, not type-expression strings such as \"list[Review]\". Each runtime tool call executes immediately in the interpreter and returns its real result to the same agent session. Tools run sequentially, not through a deferred action queue. Saving a variable takes effect immediately; prose replies do not create bindings. Functions retain their normal lexical scope and runtime guards. Any runtime or MCP tool error stops the command immediately, preserving the original failure. The model cannot continue or retry after a failed action; completed external actions are not repeated. Follow important steps with an spl check: --check validates syntax only; it cannot validate a blockquote's intent. --trace records the agent's decisions and runtime tool results. Replay restores shared-variable changes and function execution without repeating agent-owned external effects or contacting MCP. Generation-only --record cannot record blockquotes. Run only trusted documents. Old traces containing submitted SPL are incompatible with agent commands; record a new trace. Human input prompts directly in the shell and returns the answer to the same agent session. Repeat --human \u003canswer> to answer questions in order without prompting. The explicit --checkpoint/--resume workflow remains available for automation: completed decisions and effects are replayed from the journal after --resume, then the agent receives the answer and continues. See Human input.","group":"The language","signatures":["load_variable(name)","save_variable(name, value, schema?)","call_function(name, args)","get_env(name)","ask_human(question)","read_file","write_file","web_fetch","web_search","shell","restrict(kind, value)","set_guidance(kind, text)","success(value)","fail(reason)","@ENFORCE text","@FORBID text","@RESTRICT fs['./src'] exec['git']","@validate[Schema]","@HUMAN question","@SUCCESS","@fail"]},{"title":"Generation: ask and think","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#generation-ask-and-think","text":"ask prompt returns the model's answer as a value. think is the same with medium reasoning by default. Neither has tools: they cannot read files, browse or run commands. Each call starts a fresh session. Pass context explicitly, usually through interpolation. Model responses stream into the interactive CLI view and notebook cell outputs while the model is working, including available reasoning by default. hide=True suppresses reasoning, not responses. These are previews: variables, JSON parsing, schema validation and repairs use the completed response, never partial chunks. Failures and cancelled turns do not turn partial output into a successful result. Traces and recordings retain complete responses rather than streaming chunks; replay does not manufacture token timing. Embedded integrations can use LoggerOptions.streamSink for request-scoped ModelStreamEvent updates and completion/failure notifications. Backend callers can supply LlmRequest.onStream. Updates identify a response or reasoning channel and an SDK message ID: append chunks with mode: 'append', replace content with mode: 'replace', and reset the preview when the message ID changes. Ordinary event/string sinks still receive complete transcripts; LlmResponse.text is always the authoritative return value.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Agents","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#agents","text":"agent prompt lets the model act with the runtime tools (read_file, write_file, web_fetch, web_search, shell) and any configured MCP tools. Actions are checked against active restrictions. Agents default to medium reasoning and never use repairs, so a failed check never repeats their actions.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Modifiers","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#modifiers","text":"All three commands take named modifiers in parentheses before the prompt. Values are expressions, evaluated once and validated before the call. Unknown or duplicate names are syntax errors. Modifier Values Effect --- --- --- len word sentence paragraph long Length hint (guidance only) fmt see [Output formats](#output-formats) Parse and check the answer schema JSON Schema draft-07 Validate the answer; implies JSON repairs 0 – 5 Extra attempts after a format or schema error reasoning low medium high Reasoning effort, if the model supports it hide True False (default) Suppress reasoning display when True ; responses remain visible model model id Per-call model tokens Positive safe integer Maximum generated tokens per model turn, including reasoning seed Integer 0..2147483647 ask only: request repeatable sampling where supported temperature Finite number 0..2 ask only: control sampling variability top_p Finite number 0..1 ask only: control nucleus sampling The former show modifier is no longer accepted. Omit show=True (reasoning is now visible by default), and replace show=False with hide=True. tokens is a hard generation budget, not a length hint or a separate thinking budget. The same limit applies independently to every repair attempt and agent tool-continuation turn. Omitting it preserves existing provider defaults. It maps to OpenAI max_output_tokens (or max_completion_tokens for seeded calls), Anthropic max_tokens, and Google maxOutputTokens. Invalid values fail before the model call; provider/model limits still apply. Legacy Anthropic manual thinking requires tokens to exceed its reasoning budget (low: 1024, medium: 4096, high: 16384); conflicting settings fail rather than silently enlarging the generation budget. If the model hits its token limit, the call fails with llm_error, not a successful partial answer, and format/schema repairs do not retry the truncated turn. Explicit budgets are recorded in effective.tokens and participate in recording/trace request matching, so changing a budget cannot silently reuse an old answer. Model precedence: CLI --model, then the per-call model, then frontmatter model, then the provider default. OpenAI, Anthropic and Google are supported through their official clients; use bare model IDs or provider/model. See Models and authentication.","group":"The language","signatures":["len","fmt","schema","repairs","reasoning","hide","model","tokens","seed","temperature","top_p"]},{"title":"Randomness controls","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#randomness-controls","text":"Set sampling modifiers directly in ask(...): Parameters are optional named expressions, evaluated once in source order alongside other modifiers and before the prompt. The same values are reused for every repair attempt. Omitted sampling controls use provider/model defaults; no seed, temperature or top_p is invented. Sampling controls apply only to that ask expression, not to surrounding code or Markdown blockquotes. think and agent do not accept them; their existing modifiers and reasoning defaults are unchanged. ask has no implicit reasoning effort. An explicit reasoning='low', 'medium' or 'high' always takes precedence. Models that cannot disable thinking use the SDK's lowest supported thinking setting with a compatibility warning. Parameter Accepted values Meaning --- --- --- seed Integer 0..2147483647 Request repeatable sampling where the provider supports it temperature Finite number 0..2 Lower values reduce variability; 0 requests greedy sampling where supported top_p Finite number 0..1 Nucleus sampling; smaller values narrow token choices ( 1 does not narrow them) Unknown/duplicate names, or sampling modifiers on think or agent, are syntax errors. Out-of-range values, booleans, strings and None are runtime errors before the model call. The former randomness(...) prefix and eval(...) statement are no longer supported. Move sampling values into ask(...) and use assert and schemas for explicit output checks. Provider limitations are diagnosed with warnings; the runtime keeps supported controls without changing the model, and preserves explicitly requested reasoning: The response's effective.randomness records controls actually retained by the provider adapter. For sampling controls, null means the control was omitted/unsupported and provider defaults apply, not that a particular default numeric value was sent. effective.reasoningEffort is none when disabling thinking is honored, or null when the adapter cannot honor that setting; null does not mean thinking is disabled. Traces, recordings, checkpoints and pinned workflows include requested sampling controls in request identity; changing them cannot silently reuse an old response. Calls without sampling controls or tokens retain compatibility with existing replay files. Recordings retain the same sampling request shape, but recreate pins and checkpoints after editing their source documents. Even extreme settings cannot guarantee identical outputs from hosted models. Use --pin for fully deterministic offline replay. See the official OpenAI, Google and Anthropic adapter documentation.","group":"The language","signatures":["seed","temperature","top_p"]},{"title":"Output formats","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#output-formats","text":"fmt Accepts --- --- text (default) Trimmed text, no parsing integer JSON number without a fraction float Any finite JSON number boolean JSON true or false array JSON array (a list) object JSON object (a dict, not an array) json Any JSON value Parsing is strict: prose around JSON and Markdown code fences are rejected, and values are never coerced. A malformed or wrong-typed answer raises invalid_format.","group":"The language","signatures":["text","integer","float","boolean","array","object","json"]},{"title":"Schemas and repairs","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#schemas-and-repairs","text":"schema accepts SPL type descriptors or raw JSON Schema. Both reject invalid output without coercion, defaults or removal of extra fields. A mismatch raises validation_failed with field paths. Use enums, structs and aliases to describe typed output directly: Struct fields are exact and required, including nullable fields. Metadata in brackets is model advice only, not a data constraint. Raw JSON Schema still uses strict Ajv validation when constraints such as minimum or minLength must be enforced: Raw schemas validate dynamic JSON values; they do not install SPL type contracts or hydrate classes. Descriptor targets decode typed representations, including exact signed-64-bit integer tokens and distinct finite floats. Legacy fmt='integer' and fmt='float' alone still return dynamic numbers; use schema=int or schema=float when a typed numeric result is required. repairs=n allows up to n more attempts, each with the previous answer and the validation errors. Only invalid_format and validation_failed are repaired; transport and tool errors are not. The prompt is not re-evaluated. Put business rules in assert, after the call. Struct targets decode validated structural data; schema=Person hydrates a nominal instance only for a hydratable class Person. schema=struct(Person) instead returns structural field data, never a Person instance. A binding annotation checks the result and does not secretly configure the ask target. Typed JSON decoding preserves signed-64-bit tokens exactly; typed decoding or complete hydration failures use the same repair budget (zero by default, at most five extra attempts). Unsupported non-JSON schemas and ambiguous distinct targets fail before provider calls. See class schemas and migration.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Guidance: enforce and forbid","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#guidance-enforce-and-forbid","text":"enforce text: and forbid text: introduce indented blocks and add instructions to every model call inside, including calls in functions they invoke. Nested guidance combines. Guidance shapes prompts; it does not check the answer.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Guards & failures","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html","text":"Model output varies, so SPL makes checks explicit: restrict limits actions, assert and schemas check data, failures can be caught, and human hands decisions to a person.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Restrictions","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#restrictions","text":"restrict limits what tools and agents may touch inside its block. Outside any restriction, tools and agents are allowed by default. A blocked action raises restriction_violation. Clause Allows --- --- restrict './out' or restrict fs './out' Files inside the folder only restrict net 'api.example.com' Only these hosts ( *.example.com matches subdomains) restrict exec 'npm,git' Only these executables restrict mcp 'docs/search' Only these MCP servers ( docs , docs/* ) or tools ( docs/search ) Values are a comma-separated string or a list of strings. Combine clauses with commas; nested or repeated clauses of the same kind intersect, so a scope can only narrow. What is checked: Models are told the active restrictions, and the runtime enforces them on every call. ask and think have no tools at all.","group":"The language","signatures":["restrict './out'","restrict fs './out'","restrict net 'api.example.com'","restrict exec 'npm,git'","restrict mcp 'docs/search'"]},{"title":"Assertions, fail and success","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#assertions-fail-and-success","text":"","group":"The language"},{"title":"Failure payload","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#failure-payload","text":"Every failure has the same shape. Uncaught failures are printed to stdout and exit with code 1. status_code Cause --- --- invalid_format Answer is not valid JSON of the requested fmt validation_failed Answer does not match the schema type_error Known typed violation before execution, or a runtime contract violation assertion_failed An assert was false explicit_failure fail was executed restriction_violation An action was blocked by restrict tool_error Missing file, HTTP failure or nonzero shell exit llm_error Authentication, timeout, transport or replay mismatch runtime_error Undefined name, wrong type, invalid modifier and similar bugs","group":"The language","signatures":["invalid_format","validation_failed","type_error","assertion_failed","explicit_failure","restriction_violation","tool_error","llm_error","runtime_error"]},{"title":"try / except","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#try--except","text":"The handler gets the failure payload as a dict. Failures do not roll back earlier writes. Syntax errors and statically known typed violations are reported before anything runs, so they cannot be caught. Runtime contract violations can be caught; failed typed bulk mutations leave the affected value unchanged.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Human in the loop","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#human-in-the-loop","text":"human 'question' asks a person and returns their answer as a string. In normal CLI runs, the shell prints the question on stderr and reads one line from stdin, preserving whitespace except the line ending. Execution continues immediately without a suspension or repeating completed tool/model calls. Use repeatable --human \u003canswer> flags to supply exact answers in question order and skip prompting. Remaining questions prompt normally, including with --silent; EOF without an answer fails the script. Blockquote agents use the ask_human tool for the same workflow (@HUMAN). The answer is returned to the same agent session, which may then make new model calls and save it to a shared variable. Runtime actions completed before the question are not repeated, including actions inside invoked functions.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Explicit checkpoint mode","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#explicit-checkpoint-mode","text":"For noninteractive automation, --checkpoint still saves progress to a journal, prints the question as JSON and exits with code 3. A later --resume invocation supplies the answer and continues without repeating completed tool or model calls. These modes never read stdin and cannot use --human. The pending question looks like this: Resume needs the same workspace, script, runtime, --model/--provider/--mcp options and environment. The journal is not a snapshot: files changed in between stay changed. If a run was interrupted mid-action, SPL refuses to guess and asks for manual recovery. Checkpoints cannot be combined with stores or trace/replay. Journals contain prompts and answers, so keep them private. In CI, treat exit code 3 as \"waiting\", publish the question to an authorized channel, and resume in the same persistent workspace once an answer arrives.","group":"The language"},{"title":"CLI & runtime","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html","text":"spl runs one Markdown workflow, prints its result on stdout and logs on stderr, and reports the outcome through its exit code. Requires Node.js >=24.15.0.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Usage","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#usage","text":"Option Description --- --- --arg \u003cvalue> Script argument exposed as $args[i] ; repeatable, also --arg=value --human \u003canswer> Answer a human question without prompting; repeatable in question order, also --human=answer --model \u003cid> Model for every call, overriding per-call and frontmatter models --provider \u003cname> openai , anthropic or google ; model IDs also accept provider/model --cwd \u003cdir> Base folder for paths, restrictions, tools and agents (default: current) --check Validate frontmatter, syntax and --mcp config without running --log-level \u003clevel> error , info (default) or debug --silent No execution logs; human prompts still appear on stderr --timeout \u003cs> Per model call (default 300) --mcp \u003cfile.json> MCP servers, see [MCP servers](#mcp-servers) --trace / --replay \u003cfile.jsonl> Record or replay model calls, see [Traces and replay](#traces-and-replay) --pin Save a successful workflow as self-contained [locked Markdown](#pinned-workflows) --store \u003cfile.json> / --store-module \u003cpath> Persist top-level variables, see [Variable stores](#variable-stores) --checkpoint , --resume , --answer-for , --answer , --answer-file [Human input](Safety.md#human-in-the-loop) -v , --version / -h , --help Version / usage Each --arg is one string; use --arg=--value for values starting with a dash. Normal runs handle human expressions and blockquote @HUMAN questions directly. The shell prints each question on stderr and reads one line from stdin, preserving whitespace except the line ending. Repeat --human to supply answers in question order without prompting: Supplied answers are exact strings, including empty or multiline values; use --human=--value for answers starting with a dash. Once supplied answers are exhausted, remaining questions prompt normally, even with --silent. EOF without an answer is a script failure, not a suspension. --human cannot be combined with explicit --checkpoint or --resume; those retain the noninteractive journal/answer workflow described in Human input.","group":"Reference","signatures":["--arg \u003cvalue>","--human \u003canswer>","--model \u003cid>","--provider \u003cname>","--cwd \u003cdir>","--check","--log-level \u003clevel>","--silent","--timeout \u003cs>","--mcp \u003cfile.json>","--trace","--replay \u003cfile.jsonl>","--pin","--store \u003cfile.json>","--store-module \u003cpath>","--checkpoint","--resume","--answer-for","--answer","--answer-file","-v","--version","-h","--help"]},{"title":"Output and exit codes","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#output-and-exit-codes","text":"stdout gets the success value: strings as-is, everything else as JSON. An uncaught failure prints its payload instead. stderr gets logs, including every model prompt and response and committed variable-state changes. Use --silent when logs could expose sensitive data. Each event has one header, without repeated continuation badges, panels or timestamps: Icon Category Color --- --- --- -> Model input: ask, think, agent or blockquote Magenta \u003c- Model output or runtime action Cyan x Error Red ! Warning Yellow i Information Blue . Debug Gray ~ Variable state Yellow Generic embedding messages have a - message header. Explicit script logs keep their [info|error|debug] log: prefix for embedding/notebook compatibility. State logs identify each affected binding's defining scope, previous value (\u003cunbound> on initialization) and new value. Parameters, loop/handler bindings, closures and shared mutations are included. A shared mutation is one event listing all affected aliases. Same-value assignments, no-op methods, reads, scope exits and initial runtime/store baselines do not create events. Automatic executed-source and control-flow step traces no longer appear at any verbosity. Historical checkpoint reconstruction is suppressed; new commits and ordinary trace replay remain visible. Terminal stdin and stderr enable an isolated BetterTUI view on stderr, even when stdout is piped. Event headers are bold when terminal styling is enabled. An animated trailing loading character remains visible while the workflow runs, including while waiting for model output. It disappears on exit and is never included in the expanded transcript or stdout. Generated model prefaces (runtime/document preambles, guidance and initial blockquote context) and every object/array start as folded summaries; the requested prompt stays visible. Up/Down selects a payload in the viewport or scrolls text; Enter/Space toggles a visible selection; Page Up/Down scrolls; Home/End reaches the ends. Other folds and selection survive arrival/resize. The view initially follows new output. Scrolling away from the bottom pauses following, not execution; scrolling back to the bottom with arrows, Page Down or End resumes following. Enter/Space never jumps to an off-screen selection, and End reaches the output tail even when text or a streaming preview extends beyond the last payload. Expanded JSON uses two-space indentation and preserves original numeric/string tokens and duplicate keys. Primitives and malformed JSON remain readable without misleading fold controls. Model responses and available reasoning stream live in this view by default; hide=True suppresses reasoning. Partial JSON is shown as text until the authoritative response arrives, when normal formatting and folding apply. Streaming previews are separate from immutable log events, so the final transcript contains each complete response once. Redirected logs remain expanded and append-only. --silent also suppresses streaming previews. Normal finish restores input/cursor/screen state and leaves one complete expanded transcript on stderr; no inspection keypress delays exit. Renderer/native failures are diagnosed and select plain append-only output without changing workflow results. Non-terminal stdin/stderr, TERM=dumb, embedded sinks, silent, help/version, auth, check-only and checkpoint/resume never acquire the UI. Before a human prompt, the interactive renderer exits and restores the terminal; subsequent logs remain append-only so only the shell reads the answer. Supplied --human answers do not stop the UI. Before native rendering, the child queries cursor visibility and alternate-screen state. Unsupported queries or an already-active alternate screen select diagnosed append-only output; SPL does not clear an existing application's alternate screen. The parent owns the input-mode snapshot and cursor restoration before native code runs, including after a child crash. Message, label and value controls are sanitized rather than executed as terminal instructions. Colors are enabled automatically when stderr is a terminal, and disabled for redirected output or TERM=dumb. Set NO_COLOR=1 to disable color, or FORCE_COLOR=1 to enable it when redirecting; FORCE_COLOR=0 disables color, and NO_COLOR takes precedence. stdout results, checkpoint envelopes, trace journals and variable stores remain plain, machine-readable data. --silent suppresses execution logs; usage, syntax and checkpoint-usage diagnostics still appear on stderr so command-line errors are not hidden. --log-level retains ordinary severity filtering; model transcripts and state events appear at every non-silent level. Forced color never enables cursor controls or keyboard input. For example, log('review:', review) produces: Embedded callers can pass structured data directly with logger.info('review:', review). Custom logger sinks default to plain text; new Logger('info', sink, { color: true }) explicitly enables styling. Non-JSON values such as errors, bigints and circular objects use Node's inspect format instead of losing error details or failing JSON serialization. An additive eventSink option delivers detached readonly events instead of strings, never both. Neither library imports nor custom sinks load BetterTUI or start a process. Automatic observation uses non-invoking snapshots: callable identities, cycle references and accessor placeholders, without running getters, serializers or custom inspectors. Code Meaning --- --- 0 Success 1 Script failure 2 Syntax or usage error 3 Waiting for human input in explicit checkpoint/resume mode 70 Internal or infrastructure error","group":"Reference","signatures":["->","\u003c-","x","!","i",".","~","0","1","2","3","70"]},{"title":"Traces and replay","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#traces-and-replay","text":"--trace writes every ask, think, agent and blockquote request with its raw response or failure to a new JSONL file. --replay feeds those results back without contacting model providers or MCP servers, so you can iterate on code and guards for free. Requests must match exactly and in order (prompt, kind, model, reasoning, display setting); a mismatch, missing or unused entry fails. Traces and recordings retain the showReasoning request field: it is now true by default, and hide=True sets it to false. To replay older entries with showReasoning:false, add hide=True to the matching calls or record a new trace. Replay is not a dry run: explicit read_file, write_file, shell and web calls, and stores, still run. Agent actions are not repeated, and their side effects are not recreated. Blockquote decisions rebuild shared bindings and run existing functions against recorded external-tool results. The older --record mode writes generation-only recordings; replaying them blocks writes, shell, network, agents (including blockquotes) and stores. Traces and recordings contain full prompts, answers and requested environment values, so keep them private.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Pinned workflows","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#pinned-workflows","text":"--pin runs the workflow normally. Only after successful execution and backend cleanup does it create a sibling flow.locked.md; the original file is unchanged, and an existing locked file is never overwritten. --check --pin only validates and writes nothing. Failed or interrupted runs do not produce a locked workflow. The locked document retains the original Markdown and appends a versioned JSON execution journal inside a double blockquote (>>) with a spl-lock fence. It includes every returned model response (including repair attempts and reasoning), blockquote runtime decisions, explicit tool outcomes and human answers. This block is data, never another instruction to a model. The runtime recognizes the journal automatically, including through the embedding run API. Locked workflows are standalone; ExecutionSession rejects them instead of mixing pinned inputs with retained state. It substitutes recorded answers and reconstructs shared state deterministically: no model/provider or MCP calls, file/network/shell operations, repeated writes, variable-store access or human prompts. The initial variable-store snapshot, script arguments, CLI model override and working directory are retained. When $env or a blockquote environment tool is used, the environment snapshot is retained as well. Replay checks source identity, execution options, request order and complete journal consumption; missing, malformed, mismatched or unused entries fail without falling back to live calls. Pinning cannot be combined with checkpoint/resume or trace/record/replay. Locked workflows also reject stores, MCP configuration and supplied human answers. Explicit argument, model or working-directory overrides must match the pinned values. To change the workflow or its inputs, run the original file with --pin again after moving or removing the old locked file. Keep locked files private. Responses, human answers, file contents, initial variables and any accessed environment snapshot can contain secrets. Pinning is not a sandbox: the original run still performs all requested actions; locked replay does not repeat those actions or recreate their effects.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Variable stores","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#variable-stores","text":"--store state.json loads top-level variables at start and saves them at exit, even after a failure. Functions and $env/$args are not saved. See journal.md. The built-in JsonFileStore writes typed numeric tokens losslessly, but its loader remains dynamic and stores do not contain descriptors. Explicit typed decoding is needed to recover new numeric representations and contracts; ordinary JSON loading is not a typed round trip. --store-module ./store.mjs uses a module exporting createStore(), which returns synchronous load() and save(vars) methods:","group":"Reference"},{"title":"MCP servers","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#mcp-servers","text":"--mcp takes a file in VS Code's mcp.json format. MCP tools are available only to agents. Use the server names in restrict mcp, and --check to validate the file without starting servers. Only load configurations you trust.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Models and authentication","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#models-and-authentication","text":"Every model call uses the official OpenAI, Anthropic or Google client in a fresh conversation. See Getting started for API-key login and environment variables. A call without credentials, or a provider/model access error, fails with llm_error. Deterministic workflows, check-only operations and replay do not initialize providers or MCP. Model precedence is unchanged: CLI --model, per-call model, frontmatter model, provider default. IDs may be bare (claude-sonnet-4-5, gemini-2.5-flash, gpt-4.1) or qualified (anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5). Recognizable bare IDs infer their provider; custom IDs can be qualified or used with --provider. Unknown models are passed unchanged to the selected provider and fail if unavailable; there is no fallback to another model. Provider selection uses explicit --provider, then the model's prefix/inferred provider, then SPL_PROVIDER, then the saved login default, then a single configured provider. An explicit provider conflicting with the model's provider is an error. Multiple configured providers without a selection are an error, not an arbitrary choice. Defaults are gpt-4.1 (OpenAI), claude-sonnet-4-5 (Anthropic), and gemini-2.5-flash (Google). SPL reasoning levels map to OpenAI reasoning effort, Anthropic thinking budgets/adaptive effort, and Google thinking budgets/levels. Unsupported recognized combinations warn and use the model default; effective provider, model and reasoning effort are retained in traces. Runtime tools execute sequentially, MCP results remain traceable, and timeout/cancellation never accepts a partial answer. Failed actions are not automatically retried. Live output remains non-reproducible; contain variance with schemas, assertions and traces.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Embedding","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#embedding","text":"run returns success (with value), failure (with failure), or waiting_for_input when given a Checkpoint. Options include backend, logger, cwd, args (default [], never process.argv), store, model, human and checkpoint. A human(question, line) handler returns a string or Promise\u003cstring> for both human expressions and blockquote ask_human calls. Embedded runs never read stdin themselves; provide this handler or a checkpoint when requesting human input. A checkpoint takes precedence over the handler and retains suspension semantics. Wrap a backend in TraceBackend to record, or use ReplayBackend to replay; close it to check for unused entries. A custom backend implements call(request) and close(); only requests of kind agent may run tools. Raw parse and run still accept plain SPL source. AiBackend accepts an optional provider alongside logger, cwd, timeoutMs and mcpServers. The previous CopilotBackend export is an alias for embedding compatibility, not a Copilot connection.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Grammar","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html","text":"The complete syntax of SPL documents and the code inside spl fences, in EBNF. Modifier names are checked while parsing; modifier values and schemas are checked at runtime.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Notation","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html#notation","text":"{ x } repeats, [ x ] is optional, | separates alternatives, and quoted text is literal. EBNF repetition braces are notation, not SPL block delimiters. Breaking change: blocks start with : on the header's line, followed by a newline and an indented, nonempty body. Replace old block braces with this layout; expand inline suites onto separate lines and use pass for empty bodies. Dictionary braces are also removed: use object: and object(). Interpolation {expr} and JSON wire syntax are unchanged. Anonymous value-producing blocks use the same bare-colon syntax. Indentation adds exactly four spaces per block level. The lexer emits INDENT when a block's indentation increases and DEDENT when it decreases, including at end of input. Tabs in indentation, unexpected indentation, missing bodies and dedents not matching a prior indentation level are syntax errors. Blank and comment-only lines are ignored and cannot satisfy a block's body. elif, else and except align with their opening statement. NL is a significant logical newline. Newlines inside () and [] suspend ordinary indentation, but explicit object suites still produce indentation tokens; a trailing \\ explicitly continues a line. # starts a comment outside strings. Semicolons separate simple statements only, not compound statements or block headers. A completed block ends at its dedent; a statement whose value ends with a block can end there without another physical newline.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Complete grammar","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html#complete-grammar","text":"","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Block expressions","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html#block-expressions","text":"The same layout works in value positions without grouping parentheses: Each block is still a lexical child scope: let declares locally, assignment searches outward, and child locals do not leak into the parent. Python-like indentation does not introduce Python's assignment semantics.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Maintaining the grammar","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html#maintaining-the-grammar","text":"This page is the only copy of the grammar. When the syntax changes, update it together with the VS Code TextMate grammar, then run npm run docs:build from the repository root. The documentation tests check that the rendered grammar and the example listings stay in sync.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Examples","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html","text":"Every example is a runnable document in lang/examples. Run them from the lang folder, for example spl examples/hi.md. Each example's header shows its arguments.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Getting started","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#getting-started","text":"Example Shows --- --- [hi.md](../lang/examples/hi.md) Hydrated class schemas, shared variables, an assertion and a restricted JSON projection [basics.md](../lang/examples/basics.md) Checking a model's answer against exact code; guidance with @ENFORCE and @FORBID [is-friday.md](../lang/examples/is-friday.md) Branching on a JSON answer [env.md](../lang/examples/env.md) Reading $env and --arg values [color-poem.md](../lang/examples/color-poem.md) Asking a person directly with @HUMAN , or supplying --human blue --human apple","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Developer workflows","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#developer-workflows","text":"Example Shows --- --- [code-review.md](../lang/examples/code-review.md) A review that fails CI on high-severity findings [release-notes.md](../lang/examples/release-notes.md) User-facing notes from git log prepended to CHANGELOG.md [triage-issues.md](../lang/examples/triage-issues.md) Enums and a named typed-list schema; labeling GitHub issues with gh after human approval","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Data and documents","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#data-and-documents","text":"Example Shows --- --- [extract-invoice.md](../lang/examples/extract-invoice.md) Struct schemas and a Money alias with integer-cent arithmetic [typed-data.md](../lang/examples/typed-data.md) Primitives, aliases, unions, validation, guarded lists, structs, classes, callable fields and deep projection without a model [translate-docs.md](../lang/examples/translate-docs.md) Translations verified to keep links, code and headings [incident-report.md](../lang/examples/incident-report.md) Exact log statistics, model-written narrative [web-digest.md](../lang/examples/web-digest.md) Web research with restricted fetches and checked citations","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Agents, state and people","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#agents-state-and-people","text":"Example Shows --- --- [mcp.md](../lang/examples/mcp.md) An agent limited to one MCP tool [journal.md](../lang/examples/journal.md) State across runs with --store [human-review.md](../lang/examples/human-review.md) Asking for review directly, with optional checkpoint/resume automation","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Extract an invoice","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#extract-an-invoice","text":"The struct schema checks the shape of the answer; a reusable Money alias keeps arithmetic in signed-64-bit integer cents. Metadata such as minimum=0 is model advice, not a constraint; plain arithmetic checks that the numbers add up.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Triage issues","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#triage-issues","text":"Exact gh calls fetch and label issues; the model only classifies, and nothing changes until a person approves.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Typing migration","page":"Typing migration","href":"migration.html","text":"This is a breaking source revision, including for unannotated programs. Migrate source manually; there is no legacy grammar mode or general-purpose automatic converter. Recreate pins and checkpoints after editing their source. The full contract is recorded in TYPES.md.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Blocks and dictionaries","page":"Typing migration","href":"migration.html#blocks-and-dictionaries","text":"Old blocks such as if True { log('ok') } are rejected. Replace the opening brace with a colon, put the body on new lines, and remove the closing brace. Each level adds exactly four spaces; tabs, inline suites and inconsistent dedents are errors. Align elif, else and except with their opening statement. Use pass for an otherwise empty statement body. Old dictionaries such as let data = {'name': 'Ada'} are also rejected. Use an object: expression; replace {} with object(). Do not change ordinary JavaScript objects, raw JSON wire payloads, or interpolation braces inside SPL strings. Expression continuation inside () and [] suspends ordinary indentation; explicit object: blocks still delimit their own entries. Object keys do not introduce a lexical scope. Inline def properties capture ordinary lexical variables, receive no implicit self, and should access fields through an explicit argument or captured dictionary. Dictionary built-in methods win in member calls: o.pop() calls the built-in, whereas (o.pop)() invokes a function stored at the colliding key.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Opt into typed contracts","page":"Typing migration","href":"migration.html#opt-into-typed-contracts","text":"Untyped bindings remain dynamically assignable. Add annotations only where an enforced contract is wanted. Known typed violations stop execution before model calls; unknown dynamic values are checked at runtime without coercion or repair. Structs are structural, exact records: every field is required, including nullable fields. Missing and extra fields fail. Enums accept matching strings directly. char requires exactly one Unicode scalar, not one UTF-16 code unit or grapheme cluster. fn(T1, T2) -> R uses explicit arity, contravariant parameters and covariant returns; annotated and guarded callable boundaries check arguments and results. Neither validate(value, Type) nor isinstance(value, Type) converts legacy numbers. Explicit validate(data, Class) can turn structural field data into a nominal instance; isinstance remains a non-converting test. Field advice such as str [min=2, desc='Name'] is compile-time JSON-compatible metadata, not a constraint: a one-character string still satisfies that type. Raw JSON Schema constraints remain strict. Forward declarations, recursive data schemas and inheritance are not supported.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Reusable type aliases","page":"Typing migration","href":"migration.html#reusable-type-aliases","text":"Use type Name = T for a read-only, transparent alias. Aliases work in annotations, fields, unions, lists and schema= targets, preserving the underlying representation and class identity: Advice is descriptive, not a constraint: minimum=0 does not reject negative integers. Assert business rules explicitly. Alias chains inherit advice; newly declared keys override inherited keys, while field advice is appended separately. type(value) still returns the runtime type name. For blockquotes, give composite schemas a binding name, for example type Batch = list[Request], then request @validate[Batch]. The agent passes schema=\"Batch\" to save_variable; a string such as \"list[Request]\" is not a declared binding name.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Numeric representations and conversions","page":"Typing migration","href":"migration.html#numeric-representations-and-conversions","text":"Typed 1 is a signed 64-bit int; typed 1.0 is a distinct finite binary64 float. Untyped literals retain legacy number behavior. A legacy value needs explicit int(value) or float(value) conversion to enter a new representation; validation never converts it. int(...) is exact and range-checked: fractions, booleans, nonfinite values, out-of-range inputs, and inexact integer strings fail. It no longer truncates fractions or treats booleans as integers. The inclusive range is -9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854775807. float(...) constructs the distinct representation and rejects NaN and infinities; converting an integer to binary64 may round and lose precision. Mixed int/float arithmetic requires explicit conversion. Integer arithmetic checks overflow; integer / produces a float. sum, abs and round preserve typed numeric representations. Ordinary len and range counts remain legacy numbers: use explicit int(...) when a typed count is required. Descriptor-directed JSON decoding preserves integer tokens without first passing through a JavaScript number. For int | float, integer tokens select int, decimal/exponent tokens select float. SPL emits integers exactly and integral floats as 1.0 (or exponent spelling). External JSON normalization may erase that distinction; only SPL's typed encoder/decoder provides this round-trip guarantee. JsonFileStore writes typed numeric tokens losslessly, but loading a store remains dynamic: files do not carry type descriptors or automatically restore typed contracts. Use an explicitly requested descriptor for typed numeric decoding; do not assume a generic JSON loader preserves signed-64-bit values or the int/float distinction.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Class schemas versus structural data","page":"Typing migration","href":"migration.html#class-schemas-versus-structural-data","text":"Classes are nominal instances with declared fields, a constructor named after the class, and implicitly bound self in constructors and methods. A matching dictionary is not a class instance. Normal construction runs the constructor and must initialize every declared field before publishing the instance. Direct schema hydration requires hydratable class; explicit validate(data, Class) can populate an ordinary class from validated fields without running the constructor. schema=Person returns a hydrated instance only when the class opts in. schema=struct(Person) returns validated structural field data, not a Person. Consequently the old proposed let result: Person = ask(schema=struct(Person)) 'Random Person' cannot satisfy the annotation. An annotation never secretly selects an ask schema, format or hydration target. To convert the structural result explicitly, use let person: Person = validate(data, Person). This validates exact declared fields, recursively converts nested class data and bypasses constructors. It does not convert legacy numbers; use typed schemas or explicit numeric conversions before validation. For an existing instance, validate(object(person), struct(Person)) creates a deep field snapshot and checks its structural contract. Dynamic object(person) projection drops named function properties; validation then rejects any missing required callable field rather than silently accepting an incomplete record. Hydration skips the constructor: methods must not assume constructor normalization, side effects or semantic invariants occurred. Callable/non-JSON required fields make a schema target unsupported before any model call; they are not silently omitted. Distinct ambiguous decoding targets fail rather than being selected by union declaration order. Typed decoding and complete hydration validation share the ask repair pipeline: zero repairs by default, at most five additional attempts, and existing format/repair restrictions. Ordinary assignment, call, mutation and validation failures do not trigger model retries.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Persistent mutation contracts and projection","page":"Typing migration","href":"migration.html#persistent-mutation-contracts-and-projection","text":"Typed mutable values retain their contracts for their lifetime, including mutations through untyped aliases, member/index writes, captured bindings and built-in mutators. Mutable contracts are invariant: list[Color] is not interchangeable with list[str]. Successful mutable isinstance tests atomically install compatible contracts, just as validation does. Failed tests leave data and contracts unchanged. Incompatible typed aliases fail even when an empty collection's contents happen to satisfy both types. Bulk writes validate everything before committing; failures leave no valid prefix written or partial contracts installed. Narrowing expires after relevant assignment or potentially mutating calls; use stable local snapshots when appropriate. Project class instances explicitly before JSON output. object(value) makes a deep dynamic snapshot without sharing mutable children or changing its source. Named function properties, including methods, are dropped by dynamic projection; root functions, array functions and cycles are rejected. Class-to-struct projection likewise snapshots declared data fields without silently discarding required non-JSON fields. Ordinary untyped function serialization is unchanged: projection rules do not globally make the serializer ignore functions.","group":"Guides"}];
